                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#conntents Conntents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relationship_to_singleton_covers"} Relationship to singleton covers
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#compatibility_of_sheafification"} Compatibility of sheafification
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong superextensive site
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            whose covering families can be reduced to single covers combined with stable coproducts. Many naturally occuring sites are superextensive. The prefix ‘super-’ is opposite to ‘sub-’ as in ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"}> subset
            ’ and indicates that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            of the site contains the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+topology"} extensive topology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcanonical+site"} subcanonical site
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a finitely
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is (finitely)
            %strong superextensive
            if it includes the (finitely) extensive topology (generated by families of injections into finite coproducts) and every covering family is generated by a finite one.
          %p
            Likewise, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is infinitary-extensive, a topology is infinitary-superextensive if it includes the infinitary-extensive topology (generated by families of injections into small coproducts) and every covering family is generated by a small one. (Of course, the last condition is vacuous if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is small.)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              Well known examples of infinitary superextensive sites include
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
              with their usual topologies, generated by the open covers.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+topology"} coherent topology
              on any (finitely or infinitary) extensive coherent category is (finitely or infinitary) superextensive. In particular, this includes (in the infinitary case) the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+topology"} canonical topology
              on a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relationship_to_singleton_covers Relationship to singleton covers
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a single morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            that generates a covering family, we call it a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (j_i: U_i \to B)_i
            with the single map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [j_i]_i: \coprod_i U_i \to B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \to \coprod_i U_i
          %p
            Note that covers are stable under pullback, whenever such pullbacks exist. Even without pullbacks, they always satisfy the condition that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon U\to B
            is a cover and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon A\to B
            is any morphism, then there is a cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{f}\colon V\to A
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \bar{f}
            factors through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi j
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\bar{f}_j\colon V_j\to A)
            such that each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \bar{f}_j
            factors through
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi j
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\bar{f}_j]\colon \coprod_j V_j \to A
            is the required cover. Covers are also easily seen to be stable under composition, and generate another Grothendieck topology (which is a “singleton pretopology”). If the original topology was denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi cov
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
          %p The main conclusion is:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A superextensive Grothendieck topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              on an extensive category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is generated by the union of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi cov
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
              and the extensive topology
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By definition,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi cov
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi ext
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
              are sub-topologies of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (j_i: U_i \to B)_i
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is the composite of the family of coproduct injections
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (j_i: U_i \to \coprod_j U_j)_i
              with the cover
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi j
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [j_i]_i: \coprod_j U_j \to B
          %p
            Beware, however, of the following common misconception:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is
            %em not
            identical with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi cov
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
            %em determined by
            its singleton covers, in the sense that it can be recovered uniquely by knowing only them, it is not
            %em generated by
            its singleton covers as a Grothendieck topology; we need to also include
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi ext
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
            in the generating covering families.
          %p
            An easy way to see that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi cov
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
            must be different is to notice that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi cov
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+site"} locally connected site
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi cov
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi ext
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi ext
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
          %h3#compatibility_of_sheafification Compatibility of sheafification
          %p
            In general, it is not true, for two Grothendieck topologies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            on the same category, that
            = succeed "-sheafification" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            = succeed "-sheaves" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            = succeed "-sheaves)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∪
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1\cup T_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+arrow"} walking arrow
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            be generated by the nonidentity morphism (so that
            = succeed "-sheaves" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to 1
            is an isomorphism), and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            be generated by the empty family covering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            (so that
            = succeed "-sheaves" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is terminal).
          %p
            However, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            is the extensive topology and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi cov
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2 = T_{cov}
            for some superextensive topology (which is then recovered as
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∪
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1\cup T_2
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a finitary superextensive site, and suppose that finite coproducts of covers in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              are again covers. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a presheaf on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              which is a
              = succeed "-sheaf," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
              = succeed "-sheafification" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi cov
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is also a
              = succeed "-sheaf," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
              = succeed "-sheaf." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              We show that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "-sheaf," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^+
              is the reflection into
              = succeed "-separated" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi cov
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{++}
              is
              = succeed "-sheafification," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi cov
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi cov
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
              is generated by single covers, for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi colim
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi d
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                    %mi gl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+(c) = colim_{f\colon d \to c} gl(X,f)
            %p
              where the colimit is over all covers with target
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi gl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} gl(X,f)\subseteq X(d)
              consists of those
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X(d)
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi r
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi s
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r^*(x) = s^*(x)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi c
                    %mi d
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r,s)\colon d\times_c d \rightrightarrows d
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %p
              The extensive topology is generated by the empty covering family of the initial object, and by the injections into binary coproducts. First consider the initial object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              being a sheaf for the empty family on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(0)=1
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  terminal
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/terminal+set"} ?
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is extensive, its initial object is strict, so any morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon d\to 0
              is an isomorphism. Therefore,
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+(0) = X(0) = 1
            %p
              Now consider a binary coproduct
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = c_1 + c_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              being a sheaf for this means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(c) \to X(c_1)\times X(c_2)
              is an isomorphism (since coproducts are disjoint and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(0)
              is terminal, there is no compatibility condition to worry about). We want to show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+(c) \to X^+(c_1)\times X^+(c_2)
              is also an isomorphism.
            %p
              First suppose given
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,x'\in X^+(c)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'
              by some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x}, \bar{x'}\in X(d)
              for the same cover
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi d
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon d\to c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is extensive and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c=c_1+c_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              decomposes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              as
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = d_1+d_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_2
              are the pullbacks of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              along the two inclusions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                      %mo ←
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1 \to c \leftarrow c_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1\colon d_1\to c_1
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_2\colon d_2\to c_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_2
              can be represented by the restrictions of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x'}
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_2
              respectively.
            %p
              Now if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'
              become equal in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+(c_1)
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+(c_2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_i\colon b_i \to d_i
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=1,2
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi g
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi g
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_i^*(\bar{x}) = g_i^*(\bar{x'})
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(b_i)
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=1
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1+g_2\colon b_1+b_2 \to d_1+d_2 = d
              is a cover, and moreover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi g
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_i^*(\bar{x})
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi g
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_i^*(\bar{x'})
              can be obtained by first restricting to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_1+b_2
              and then along the two coproduct injections.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi b
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi b
                              %mn 1
                            %mo +
                            %msub
                              %mi b
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo +
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi d
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{b_i &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; b_1+b_2\\
                    ^{g_i}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{g_1+g_2}\\
                    d_i&amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; d\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                    c_i &amp; \underset{}{\to} &amp; c}
            %p
              But since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "-sheaf" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi g
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi g
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_i^*(\bar{x}) = g_i^*(\bar{x'})
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=1,2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x'}
              become equal in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(b_1+b_2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_1+b_2 \to d \to c
              is a cover, it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'
              are equal in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+(c)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+(c) \to X^+(c_1)\times X^+(c_2)
              is injective, i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+
              is
              = succeed "-separated." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
            %p
              The case of surjectivity is easier. Suppose given
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i\in X^+(c_i)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x_i}\in X(d_i)
              for some covers
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i\colon d_i\to c_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1+f_2 \colon d_1+d_2 \to c_1+c_2 = c
              is a cover. And since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "-sheaf," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x}\in X(d)
              which restricts to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x_i}
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=1,2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{x}
              represents some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X^+(c)
              which restricts to each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+
              is a
              = succeed "-sheaf." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ext
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{ext}
          %p
            Of course, there is an analogous result for infinitary superextensive sites. With a little more work we can also prove the same thing for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            and stackification.
          %p
            This result is especially interesting because sheaves and stacks for the extensive topology are easier to come by than those for some other topologies. For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is an extensive
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            represents a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}\to Cat
            which is a stack for the extensive topology, but not usually for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+topology"} coherent topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi cov
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{cov}
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+coverage"} regular topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            relative to the regular topology is still a stack for the extensive topology, and hence also for the coherent topology.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Chapter 1 of
              = succeed "\u2018" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"} David Roberts
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctors"} anafunctors
              = succeed "." do
                %em Internal categories, anafunctors and localisations
          %p
            Some discussion about terminology in this entry is on the nForum
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://nforum.mathforge.org/discussion/486/superextensive-site/?Focus=47656#Comment_47656"} here
        .revisedby
